That December, he let Jensen Huang’s company sell advanced AI chips to China, with the U.S. government taking a cut of the sales. When Trump took Huang and other business leaders to Beijing in May, Marco Rubio warned him again on the way there. Huang admitted the affair on Sunday. “I really believe him,” Trump said the next day, and Nvidia’s board kept Huang in his job.

Then reports of two more affairs came out, one with an executive who reported to him, and Huang resigned today. He ran Nvidia for 33 years without naming a successor, so its chief financial officer is now also its chief executive. Republican senators want the commerce secretary to explain in public why his department never blacklisted the company she worked for. Trump said it still isn’t clear she was a spy, then suggested Tim Cook, who just stepped down as Apple’s chief executive, to run Nvidia.

